= Askew's Dilemma

[? if hoover_memorandum_seen: Following the disastrous fallout from the Banking Crisis, Askew has successfully managed to secure key concessions from the <span style="color: #0A3161;">Western</span> <span style="color: #C8102E;">Allies</span> regarding debts and the Commonwealth's economic maneuverability. However, the Commonwealth's economic crisis continues to worsen, and the Beneduce Report has stated that the Commonwealth cannot afford War Loans payments in the foreseeable future. The <span style="color: #C8102E;">British</span> government has invited the <span style="color: #0A3161;">Western</span> <span style="color: #C8102E;">Allies</span> to participate in an international War Loans congress in Lausanne, as per the recommendations of the bankers. ?][? if war_loans == -2 and not hoover_memorandum_seen: With the moratorium on War Loans in place, there is considerable uncertainty regarding what will happen to the Commonwealth's War Loans burden once it expires. Askew argues that this uncertainty has stifled investor confidence and hindered the Commonwealth's recovery from the economic crisis. The dire state of Commonwealth finances has also contributed to this, with the Beneduce Report painting a bleak picture of the Commonwealth's ability to repay War Loans in the foreseeable future. The <span style="color: #C8102E;">British</span> government has invited the <span style="color: #0A3161;">Western</span> <span style="color: #C8102E;">Allies</span> to participate in an international War Loans congress in Lausanne, as per the recommendations of the bankers. ?]

This is where the dilemma arises: <span style="color: #002654;">Ethia</span>'s government cannot discuss any permanent revision of the Young Plan until after their elections in May. Askew faces a choice: he can either push for a prolongation of the moratorium on War Loans for another three years, or he can wait until June for a potential complete abolition of War Loans.

- @lausanne: Askew has decided to postpone reparation talks until June.

@lausanne
subtitle: A lot can happen between now and then.

Askew’s decision to postpone the Lausanne conference until June faces sharp criticism from top economic advisors, who argue that the outright abolition of War Loans would remain impossible even then. Instead, they urge him to pursue détente and secure access to new <span style="color: #002654;">Ethian</span> loans—an idea DuFour has adamantly opposed, vowing to resign rather than sign such an agreement.

Askew, however, has reason to believe waiting is the right move. <span style="color: #002654;">Ethia</span> is facing a severe fiscal shortfall and remains reluctant to cut off a crucial revenue stream.

That is, if Askew can hold on until June.